# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-batch-timeout-not-enforced -- alfe --mode batch --timeout 180 waited 20+ minutes on a BricsCAD that never answered
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-25

* Symptom

probe:template-defaults:bricscad-metric:windows (job 17450398554, retry of
17449031341, commit 6f1550bc; PF5S26B, BricsCAD V25):

: run-probes: template Default-m.dwt -> /c/Users/PPBN02261/AppData/Local/Bricsys/BricsCAD/V25x64/fr_FR/Templates/Default-m.dwt
: runner : ".../alfe-sbcl.exe" --no-init --bricscad --mode batch --timeout 180 -l "...run-probes.lsp"
: output : probe-results/bricscad/ms-windows/20261025T175056Z/results.sexp

then nothing: still running 20 minutes later, when it was cancelled by
hand (it held the Windows runner, and every CAD job queued behind it).
alfe's --timeout 180 should have given up after 3 minutes, killed the CAD
and failed the run.

The same job on the imperial template (Default-imperial.dwt, job
17449031340) and on V26 macOS (Default-m.dwt, 17449031343) completed in a
couple of minutes. What BricsCAD V25 shows on Default-m.dwt is unknown (a
modal dialog is the usual suspect: alfe-cad-nil-catch-all-message,
empty-ressource).

* To do

1. Find why the timeout did not fire: which phase alfe was in (launch /
   READY wait / script run), and whether that phase's wait honours
   --timeout.
2. Make every wait honour it, and on expiry kill the CAD it launched
   (cf. alfe-windows-old-exe-left-suspended) and fail with a message naming
   the phase.
3. Then retry the V25 metric template job: its set goes into cador's
   default objects as (:bricscad :metric 25) (MR !489 falls back to V26's).


Resolved (2026-10-25, not an alfe bug; probe-template-lookup)
-------------------------------------------------------------

Misdiagnosed. The job's full trace (timestamps UTC) shows alfe's timeout
DID fire, and the job was not cancelled but failed on its own at 18:06
(19:07 Paris):

: 17:50:56 run-probes: env - PATH=...
: 18:03:41 run-probes: template Default-m.dwt -> .../V25x64/fr_FR/Templates/Default-m.dwt
: 18:03:42   runner : ".../alfe-sbcl.exe" --no-init --bricscad --mode batch --timeout 180 ...
: 18:06:44 alfe[warn]: backend BRICSCAD: READY timeout after 180.46 s; last status = "BOOTING"
: 18:06:44 alfe[warn]: backend BRICSCAD: start aborted; terminating spawned engine (pid 7836)
: 18:06:44 alfe: ... BricsCAD did not reach READY within 180 s (last status: "BOOTING";
:          launcher exited with code 0). [READY-TIMEOUT]

The 12m45s before the runner line were run-probes.sh's template lookup:
FIND_TEMPLATE walked each whole root (%LOCALAPPDATA%, %APPDATA%, Program
Files, ProgramData) to depth 9 and filtered on "*brics*" only afterwards
(and HEAD -N 1 does not stop FIND). The imperial job, on the same root, took
4 s: it depends on the disk cache. Fixed: PRODUCT_DIRS keeps each root's
top-level folders the product's pattern names (AppData/Local/Bricsys,
Program Files/Autodesk, BricsCAD V26.app; a root that is one already, such
as Application Support/Bricsys, as is), and FIND_TEMPLATE (and the
"templates found" listing) searches only those.

What remains -- BricsCAD V25 stays BOOTING on Default-m.dwt while it reaches
READY in seconds on Default-imperial.dwt -- is
issues/open/bricscad-v25-metric-template-stays-booting.issue.
